C++ Multiple Inheritance – Resolving Function Name Conflict

Description

This C++ program demonstrates multiple inheritance and how to resolve a function name conflict when two base classes have functions with the same name.

Here:

- "Academic" has a "display()" function.
- "Sports" also has a "display()" function.
- "Student" inherits from both classes.

Class Structure

Academic       Sports
    \           /
     \         /
       Student

Academic Class

The "Academic" class contains a public "display()" function.

void display() const {
    std::cout << "Academic information\n";
}

It displays academic information.

Sports Class

The "Sports" class also contains a "display()" function.

void display() const {
    std::cout << "Sports information\n";
}

It displays sports information.

Student Class

The "Student" class inherits from both "Academic" and "Sports".

Since both base classes have a function named "display()", calling "display()" directly from a "Student" object would create ambiguity.

To solve this, the scope resolution operator "::" is used.

Academic::display();
Sports::display();

This explicitly tells the compiler which base class function should be called.

The "displayAll()" function calls both functions.

Main Function

A "Student" object is created:

Student student;

The following calls explicitly select the required "display()" function:

student.Academic::display();
student.Sports::display();

Then:

student.displayAll();

calls both functions through the "Student" class.

Output

Academic information
Sports information
Academic information
Sports information

OOP Concept Used

Multiple Inheritance with Function Ambiguity Resolution

The scope resolution operator "::" is used to specify which base class's function should be called.

Academic::display()
Sports::display()
